<section class="section"><div class="container article-layout">
  <article class="prose article" data-article>
    <div style="margin-bottom: 30px; border-radius: 12px; overflow: hidden; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0,0,0,0.06); border: 1px solid var(--border); background: #0f172a;">
      <img src="{{ asset('assets/img/blogs/signs-application-needs-modernization.svg') }}" alt="Five signs your business application needs modernization" style="width: 100%; max-height: 440px; object-fit: cover; display: block;">
    </div>
    <p>Software rarely fails in a single dramatic moment. It slowly becomes harder to change, slower to use and riskier to run. These are the signs we look for.</p>
<h2 id="1-small-changes-take-a-long-time">1. Small changes take a long time</h2>
<p>If a simple field or report takes weeks because nobody is sure what else it will break, the code has become fragile. This is usually a sign of missing tests, tangled logic or both.</p>
<h2 id="2-the-platform-is-out-of-support">2. The platform is out of support</h2>
<p>Old language and framework versions stop receiving security fixes. Running them is a risk, and finding developers willing to work on them gets harder every year.</p>
<h2 id="3-users-have-built-workarounds">3. Users have built workarounds</h2>
<p>When staff keep a parallel spreadsheet because the application cannot do what they need, the application is no longer the source of truth. That is a business risk as well as a technical one.</p>
<h2 id="4-performance-keeps-getting-worse">4. Performance keeps getting worse</h2>
<p>Pages and reports that were once instant now take a long time. Often the cause is data growth meeting queries that were never designed for it, which can frequently be improved without a full rewrite.</p>
<h2 id="5-knowledge-is-concentrated-in-one-person">5. Knowledge is concentrated in one person</h2>
<p>If only one developer, or nobody, understands how the system works, every outage becomes an emergency. Documentation and handover become part of the modernization work.</p>
<h2 id="what-to-do-next">What to do next</h2>
<p>A full rewrite is rarely the first answer. A better path usually begins with an assessment: review the code, data and hosting; identify the biggest risks; then modernise in stages, protecting the parts that work with tests as you go.</p>
<p>Our <a href="/services/application-modernization">application modernization service</a> starts with exactly that kind of assessment, with the findings written up in plain language.</p></article>
